# Digilent Basys 3 (Xilinx XC7A35T-1CPG236C).
#
# PROVENANCE: every pin name below is a line of Digilent's published
# `Basys-3-Master.xdc` (github.com/Digilent/digilent-xdc), fetched and
# transcribed rather than recalled. HIGH confidence that they are the
# balls those signals are wired to on a Basys 3 rev B, and that they are
# IO balls of the CPG236 package. See `src/fpga/devices/xc7.dev` for the
# rest of the list and for what else about this part is and is not
# verified. NOTHING HERE HAS BEEN RUN ON A BOARD.
#
# W5 is the board's single 100 MHz oscillator. There is no other clock
# source and no crystal socket.
#
# B18 and A18 are the FPGA's side of the on-board USB-UART bridge, which
# the host sees as a serial port: the FPGA RECEIVES on B18 (Digilent
# calls it `RsRx`) and TRANSMITS on A18 (`RsTx`). Note that this is the
# opposite naming from the iCE40 breakout file next to this one, where
# the pins are named from the FPGA's side; the pins themselves are what
# matters and the mapping below is FPGA transmit -> A18.
#
# The board has no reset button wired to a dedicated pin. soc_top needs
# none — it resets itself when the FPGA is configured — but if you want
# one, the five pushbuttons are U18 (centre), T18 (up), W19 (left), T17
# (right) and U17 (down), all active high with no external pull.
#
# CLK_DIV: soc_top divides the clock for its UART. At 100 MHz and 115200
# baud that is 868, not the 104 the module defaults to for a 12 MHz
# board, so build this with `-p CLK_DIV=868` or the serial output is
# unreadable.
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 clk W5
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 uart_tx A18
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 uart_rx B18
create_clock -name sys -period 10.0 clk
clock_domain sys clk

# The rest of the board, for a design that wants it. These are comments
# because this project's top has no such ports and a constraint naming a
# port the design lacks is an error; uncomment the lines your top
# declares. The pin names are the same transcription as above.
#
# Sixteen slide switches, sw[0] at the left-hand end of the row:
#   V17 V16 W16 W17 W15 V15 W14 W13 V2 T3 T2 R3 W2 U1 T1 R2
# Sixteen LEDs above them, led[0] at the same end:
#   U16 E19 U19 V19 W18 U15 U14 V14 V13 V3 W3 U3 P3 N3 P1 L1
# The four-digit seven-segment display: segments a..g are
#   W7 W6 U8 V8 U5 V5 U7, the decimal point is V7, and the four digit
#   anodes are U2 U4 V4 W4. Segments and anodes are both ACTIVE LOW.
# Pmod headers, pins 1-4 then 7-10 of each:
#   JA  J1 L2 J2 G2 H1 K2 H2 G3
#   JB  A14 A16 B15 B16 A15 A17 C15 C16
#   JC  K17 M18 N17 P18 L17 M19 P17 R18
#   JXADC J3 L3 M2 N2 K3 M3 M1 N1
# PS/2: clock C17, data B17, both wanting a pull-up.
#
# VIDEO. The Basys 3 has NO HDMI AND NO DVI CONNECTOR. Its video output
# is VGA at twelve bits, four per channel, through a resistor ladder to
# a DE-15 socket:
#   red   G19 H19 J19 N19      (bit 0 first)
#   green J17 H17 G17 D17
#   blue  N18 L18 K18 J18
#   hsync P19   vsync R19
# The `ip/dvi_tx` package in this repository drives TMDS differential
# pairs and CANNOT drive this board. Its `video_timing` module, which is
# counters and sync generation and nothing else, is reusable unchanged
# for a VGA path; the serialiser and the TMDS encoder are not.
